r)","argumentHint":"[--brief | --full | <topic> | --brief <topic>]"},{"name":"gsd-import","description":"Ingest external plans with conflict detection against project decisions before writing anything. (user)","argumentHint":"--from <filepath> | --from-gsd2"},{"name":"gsd-inbox","description":"Triage and review open GitHub issues and PRs against project templates and contribution guidelines. (user)","argumentHint":"[--issues] [--prs] [--label] [--close-incomplete] [--repo owner/repo]"},{"name":"gsd-ingest-docs","description":"Bootstrap or merge a .planning/ setup from existing ADRs, PRDs, SPECs, and docs in a repo. (user)","argumentHint":"[path] [--mode new|merge] [--manifest <file>] [--resolve auto|interactive]"},{"name":"gsd-manager","description":"Interactive command center for managing multiple phases from one terminal (user)","argumentHint":"[--analyze-deps]"},{"name":"gsd-map-codebase","description":"Analyze codebase with parallel mapper agents to produce .planning/codebase/ documents (user)","argumentHint":"[--fast [--focus tech|arch|quality|concerns]] [--query <term>|status|diff|refresh] [area]"},{"name":"gsd-mempalace-capture","description":"File a phase artifact into MemPalace; mirror decision facts into its temporal KG (user)","argumentHint":"[CONTEXT.md|PLAN.md|SUMMARY.md]"},{"name":"gsd-mempalace-recall","description":"Recall decisions, patterns, and surprises from MemPalace before planning (user)","argumentHint":"[phase-slug]"},{"name":"gsd-milestone-summary","description":"Generate a comprehensive project summary from milestone artifacts for team onboarding and review (user)","argumentHint":"[version]"},{"name":"gsd-mvp-phase","description":"Plan a phase as a vertical MVP slice — user story, SPIDR splitting, then plan-phase (user)","argumentHint":"<phase-number>"},{"name":"gsd-new-milestone","description":"Start a new milestone cycle — update PROJECT.md and route to requirements (user)","argumentHint":"[milestone name, e.g., 'v1.1 Notifications']"},{"name":"gsd-new-project","description":"Initialize a new project with deep context gathering and PROJECT.md (user)","argumentHint":"[--auto]"},{"name":"gsd-ns-context","description":"codebase intel | map graphify docs learnings mempalace (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-ns-ideate","description":"exploration capture | explore sketch spike spec capture (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-ns-manage","description":"config workspace | workstreams thread update ship inbox (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-ns-project","description":"project lifecycle | milestones audits summary (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-ns-review","description":"quality gates | code review debug audit security eval ui (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-ns-workflow","description":"workflow | discuss plan execute verify phase progress (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-pause-work","description":"Create context handoff when pausing work mid-phase (user)","argumentHint":"[--report]"},{"name":"gsd-phase","description":"CRUD for phases in ROADMAP.md — add, insert, remove, or edit phases (user)","argumentHint":"[--insert | --remove | --edit] <phase-name-or-number>"},{"name":"gsd-plan-phase","description":"Create detailed phase plan (PLAN.md) with verification loop (user)","argumentHint":"[phase] [--auto] [--research] [--skip-research] [--research-phase <N>] [--view] [--gaps] [--skip-verify] [--prd <file>] [--ingest <path-or-glob>] [--ingest-format <auto|nygard|madr|narrative>] [--reviews] [--text] [--tdd] [--mvp]"},{"name":"gsd-plan-review-convergence","description":"Cross-AI plan convergence - replan until review concerns are resolved. (user)","argumentHint":"<phase> [--codex] [--gemini] [--claude] [--opencode] [--ollama] [--lm-studio] [--llama-cpp] [--text] [--ws <name>] [--all] [--max-cycles N]"},{"name":"gsd-pr-branch","description":"Create a clean PR branch by filtering out .planning/ commits — ready for code review (user)","argumentHint":"[target branch, default: main]"},{"name":"gsd-profile-user","description":"Generate developer behavioral profile and create Claude-discoverable artifacts (user)","argumentHint":"[--questionnaire] [--refresh]"},{"name":"gsd-progress","description":"Check progress, advance workflow, or dispatch freeform intent — the unified GSD situational command (user)","argumentHint":"[--forensic | --next [--auto] [--converge] | --do \\\"task description\\\"]"},{"name":"gsd-quick","description":"Execute a quick task with GSD guarantees (atomic commits, state tracking) but skip optional agents (user)","argumentHint":"[list | status <slug> | resume <slug> | --full] [--validate] [--discuss] [--research] [task description]"},{"name":"gsd-resume-work","description":"Resume work from previous session with full context restoration (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-review","description":"Request cross-AI peer review of phase plans from external AI CLIs (user)","argumentHint":"--phase N [--gemini] [--claude] [--codex] [--opencode] [--qwen] [--cursor] [--agy] [--all]"},{"name":"gsd-review-backlog","description":"Review and promote backlog items to active milestone (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-secure-phase","description":"Retroactively verify threat mitigations for a completed phase (user)","argumentHint":"[phase number]"},{"name":"gsd-settings","description":"Configure GSD workflow toggles and model profile (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-ship","description":"Create PR, run review, and prepare for merge after verification passes (user)","argumentHint":"[phase number or milestone, e.g., '4' or 'v1.0']"},{"name":"gsd-sketch","description":"Sketch UI/design ideas with throwaway HTML mockups, or propose what to sketch next (frontier mode) (user)","argumentHint":"[design idea to explore] [--quick] [--text] [--wrap-up] or [frontier]"},{"name":"gsd-spec-phase","description":"Clarify WHAT a phase delivers with ambiguity scoring; produces a SPEC.md before discuss-phase. (user)","argumentHint":"<phase> [--auto] [--text]"},{"name":"gsd-spike","description":"Spike an idea through experiential exploration, or propose what to spike next (frontier mode) (user)","argumentHint":"[idea to validate] [--quick] [--text] [--wrap-up] or [frontier]"},{"name":"gsd-stats","description":"Display project statistics — phases, plans, requirements, git metrics, and timeline (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"gsd-surface","description":"Toggle which skills are surfaced — apply a profile, list, or disable a cluster without reinstall (user)","argumentHint":"[list|status|profile <name>|disable <cluster>|enable <cluster>|reset]"},{"name":"gsd-thread","description":"Manage persistent context threads for cross-session work (user)","argumentHint":"[list [--open | --resolved] | close <slug> | status <slug> | name | description]"},{"name":"gsd-ui-phase","description":"Generate UI design contract (UI-SPEC.md) for frontend phases (user)","argumentHint":"[phase]"},{"name":"gsd-ui-review","description":"Retroactive 6-pillar visual audit of implemented frontend code (user)","argumentHint":"[phase]"},{"name":"gsd-ultraplan-phase","description":"[BETA] Offload plan phase to Claude Code's ultraplan cloud; review in browser and import back. (user)","argumentHint":"[phase-number]"},{"name":"gsd-undo","description":"Safe git revert. Roll back phase or plan commits using the phase manifest with dependency checks. (user)","argumentHint":"--last N | --phase NN | --plan NN-MM"},{"name":"gsd-update","description":"Update GSD to latest version with changelog display (user)","argumentHint":"[--sync | --reapply | --next | --rc]"},{"name":"gsd-validate-phase","description":"Retroactively audit and fill Nyquist validation gaps for a completed phase (user)","argumentHint":"[phase number]"},{"name":"gsd-verify-work","description":"Validate built features through conversational UAT (user)","argumentHint":"[phase number, e.g., '4'] [--ws <name>]"},{"name":"gsd-workspace","description":"Manage GSD workspaces — create, list, or remove isolated workspace environments (user)","argumentHint":"[--new | --list | --remove] [name]"},{"name":"gsd-workstreams","description":"Manage parallel workstreams — list, create, switch, status, progress, complete, and resume (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-dev-close","description":"The attended milestone close for THIS repository — the night-crew development clone, which cannot be closed the way a target repo is. Runs `night-crew dev-close grade` to grade the cycle's OKRs from the artifacts hand-run slates actually produce, walks the attested key results with the operator for a judgment, reports the deferred ones with the decision that deferred them, and recommends the release before asking the order what the cycle does next. Invoke as /nc-dev-close when this repo's milestone cards are green and every run branch is triaged. The sibling of /nc-milestone-close, which is for target repos. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-milestone-close","description":"The attended milestone close for a night-crew target repo — grade the OKRs, audit the reflections, review unmet ones through the target-vs-tool ladder, transfer the aggregate-only `night-crew milestone export` document into the night-crew clone's reference/milestones/ ledger (DESIGN §15y.2), offer tool-implicating findings into the clone's BACKLOG.md with consent per item, mark the milestone boundary, and instruct the two attended commits. Invoke as /nc-milestone-close in a target repo at the end of a milestone, once every run branch is triaged. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-morning-triage","description":"Morning ritual after an overnight run — review the run branch, merge to dev, resolve DECISIONS-NEEDED forks with the operator, record resolutions in DESIGN.md §15x, update roadmap cards and HANDOFF flags. Invoke the morning after any overnight-YYYYMMDD run, or whenever DECISIONS-NEEDED.md has open forks. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-okr-session","description":"Author the cycle's OKRs with the operator — per-team objectives and measurable key results — and write .night-crew/knowledge/okrs.md. The attended, PM-guided OKR session (DESIGN §5, §15m.47). Invoke as /nc-okr-session at the start of a planning cycle, or before a PM session when okrs.md is missing or stale. OKRs are the input the PM session (add-pm-session) traces PRDs to. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-pm-grill-back","description":"The PM session's grill-back (DESIGN §5, §15m.47) — grill the operator on a drafted PRD's gray areas, driving each through one of three doors (resolved / delegated / queued) so none is carried silently, and emit the surviving delegated/queued items as the sign-off Assumptions. Invoke as /nc-pm-grill-back after /nc-pm-session has drafted a PRD, before the sign-off table (add-batch-sign-off). Rides on the PM session; reuses the user-level grilling + domain-modeling skills via the prompts/pm-grill.md overlay. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-pm-session","description":"The attended evening PM session (DESIGN §5, §15m.47) — consume the run's intake, read the Operator Brief + OKRs + roadmap + ledger, draft a PRD whose requirements trace to a key result, and route every intake item through the three doors (fold into the PRD, graduate to the backlog, or drop with a note). Invoke as /nc-pm-session at the start of an evening once OKRs exist and the Operator Brief is written. Grill-back (/nc-pm-grill-back) and the sign-off table (add-batch-sign-off) ride on this session. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-release","description":"Cut a release — tag the current `dev` with a semver version carrying the milestone name in the tag message, then promote that tag to `main` and publish it to runtime machines. The attended release moment (DESIGN §15ad.70, §15ae.74). Invoke as /nc-release when a milestone is finished and you have decided to ship what `dev` currently points at. This skill verifies and prepares, then the operator runs `task nc:release:run` — one attended, TTY-guarded command that tags, promotes to main, and pushes; the skill itself writes nothing and, having no terminal, cannot complete the release. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-retro","description":"The attended cycle retrospective — read all six pieces of evidence a cycle leaves behind (card actuals, the per-run conflict logs, the HANDOFF closeouts, `night-crew decisions audit`, the team scorecard, and the close's OKR grades) in one sitting, report what the cycle cost and what it decided per team, render both measures of PM performance — PRD-to-KR traceability named the rating, forks-prevented reported beside it unrated (§15cc.250), offer the cycle's judgments back as candidate standing preferences with consent per item (proposing only — adoption stays the operator's own act at a terminal), and write a retro record the planning round reads. Invoke as /nc-retro after /nc-dev-close and before /nc-roadmap-round, once the milestone is closed and every run branch is triaged. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-roadmap-round","description":"The attended milestone-boundary planning round — consume the backlog and the milestone's evidence (reflections, ledger, bugs, export, OKR grades), walk promotion candidates with the operator under the DESIGN §15k bar, author the next roadmap's activities and cards, record a disposition for every walked item, and seed the next cycle's OKRs alongside the roadmap they measure (§15j.42). Invoke as /nc-roadmap-round at a milestone boundary — the current roadmap's activities are done and every run branch is triaged — to author what comes next. This is the backlog's normal destiny; mid-roadmap promotion is a different, much higher bar. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-run","description":"Start tonight's overnight run from the launch prompt /nc-slate-plan saved beside the slate — no copy-paste. Reads reference/launch-<runid>.md, refuses to start on an existing unmerged run branch without an operator answer, and then executes that prompt as the run's control loop. Invoke as /nc-run in a FRESH session once a slate is signed, optionally with a run id (/nc-run 20260721-2). /nc-run --help lists all parameters. Governed by CLAUDE.md §\"Slate workflow\". (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-scorecard","description":"Narrates the cumulative team scorecard — runs `night-crew scorecard` (the mechanics: union of committed per-run scorecard files and unsynced local run logs, red flags first, trend arrows) and reports team performance in plain language. Invoke as /nc-scorecard after a run lands or before a planning round, to see how each team is trending, whether regressions escaped, and whether any run is still waiting on triage. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-slate-plan","description":"Plan an overnight slate at the roadmap-activity level — WO-sized cards with module footprints, parallel tracks, per-card time estimates against a night budget, batch sign-off, and a ready-to-paste launch prompt. Invoke as /nc-slate-plan <activity name(s)> (e.g. /nc-slate-plan Gate and verify work), with --night <duration> (e.g. --night 8.5h) to size the slate to the night, or with no args to have the target inferred from the roadmap. /nc-slate-plan --help lists all parameters. Governed by CLAUDE.md §\"Slate workflow\". (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-spike-close","description":"Close a goal's spikes by extracting what they taught into the record the slate gate reads — the closing sitting of the spike lifecycle (DESIGN §15ce.258). After a goal's spikes are run and its corrections signed (via /nc-spike-open), this sitting gathers what the spikes confirmed and learned and writes the extraction record (Outcome / Approach used / Confirmed / Learned / Plan change) at a fixed location beside the ledger, then validates it. A spike's scripts are throwaway, so this record is a spike's only surviving output — a goal whose close leaves no record is reported by the gate and does not count as closed. Invoke as /nc-spike-close <activity> [<goal>] once /nc-spike-open's spikes are green; it closes by asking `night-crew next` where the cycle goes from there. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-spike-open","description":"Begin a goal's spike work — the opening sitting of the spike lifecycle (DESIGN §15cc.252–254, §15ce.258, B-345). Per goal it records three things — the goal, what each spike proves, and the plan — then drives the iterative run: spikes execute in order, failures are worked by the agents without pausing the sequence, unresolvables escalate to the operator the moment they occur, and after the last spike the operator reviews every agent-reached correction in one sitting. Invoke as /nc-spike-open <activity> [<goal>] after a roadmap round has authored goals; when the goal's spikes are run and reviewed, hand off to /nc-spike-close to write the extraction record the slate gate reads. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"nc-status","description":"Dynamic \"you are here\" for the night-crew cycle — reads this dev repo's cycle state (OKRs, PRD, brief, slate, run branch, open forks) and roadmap card colors, reports where we are, and recommends the next /nc-* command to run. The live counterpart to the static /nc-help map. Invoke as /nc-status at the start of a session, or any time you're unsure whether the last run is triaged, whether a slate is signed and waiting, or what to plan next. (user)","argumentHint":""},{"name":"Roadmaps","description":"Create and maintain a project roadmap as a TextUSM user story map paired with a narrative ROADMAP.md. Invoke when the user asks to add a roadmap to a project, update roadmap status after shipping/archiving a change, or review what's done / in flight / planned. Cards map 1:1 to OpenSpec changes and flip color as they progress. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["roadmaps"]},{"name":"Spec feature","description":"Add a new capability or behavior change via the full OpenSpec lifecycle — proposal, spec deltas, design, tasks, mandatory operator sign-off in plain English, implementation, atomic commits with the OpenSpec-Change trailer, archive. Invoke whenever a code, config, build-target, or dependency change is requested in a project with an openspec/ directory. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["spec-feature"]},{"name":"Spec init","description":"One-time adoption of the OpenSpec spec-first workflow in the current project. Scaffolds openspec/ via the openspec CLI, appends the spec-first discipline section to the project's CLAUDE.md from the bundled template, optionally seeds HANDOFF.md, and commits the scaffolding. Invoke when a project should adopt the OpenSpec workflow (new project, or converting from GSD/ad-hoc). (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["spec-init"]},{"name":"Spec next","description":"Advisor for OpenSpec-driven projects. Reads the project's durable state (HANDOFF.md and roadmap when present, `openspec list`, git) and produces a structured \"where we are + what I'd suggest next\" report. Does NOT invoke /spec-feature or /opsx:* — it recommends; the operator decides. Invoke at the start of a fresh session, after a natural stopping point, or any time you want to align on the next move. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["spec-next"]},{"name":"TextUSM","description":"Authoring reference for TextUSM diagrams (app.textusm.com) — user story maps and sequence diagrams. Invoke whenever creating, editing, or debugging a TextUSM source file (usm/*.txt, roadmap.txt, story maps, sequence diagrams). Encodes hard-won rendering quirks: 4-space indentation only, colon re-classification in story maps, and the four-keyword frame rule in sequence diagrams. (user)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["textusm"]},{"name":"ui-jury","description":"Runs the /ui-jury orchestrator end-to-end against a live dev URL. Spawns the Cartographer as Phase 0 (Step 7.5) to discover routes and build app-map.yaml with freshness-skip logic (PIP-03/PIP-05), validates .ui-jury/hooks.yaml, creates a fresh run directory, writes the initial MANIFEST.json, invokes the user's db:reset hook (per docs/db-reset-contract.md), then spawns the Seeder subagent (Phase 08 SDR-03 — pure planner, no MCP) to emit seed-plan.json grouping states by fixture_id (Phase A), then loops the fixture groups, per iteration calling db_seed:<fixture_id> (per docs/db-seed-contract.md) and spawning the Driver subagent (Playwright-MCP-scoped) scoped to that group's state_ids (Phase B) to capture artifact bundles, then spawns 3 Critic subagents in PARALLEL — hierarchy (visual-hierarchy lens), mobile (393×852 mobile-viewport lens), correctness (console+network NDJSON lens, no Playwright) — to emit per-critic findings (D-08), then spawns the Verifier subagent (calibrated judge) to return the REPORT.md body as text — the orchestrator persists REPORT.md itself (E2 fix — host runtime refuses subagent Write for report files). Captures NDJSON events to run.log; reports the kept-count summary + REPORT.md path on completion. On any agent failure the run dir contains a structured error in MANIFEST.json and the skill exits non-zero. NO REPORT.md ships on agent failure (D-FAIL-01). (user)","argumentHint":"<dev-url> [--backend-log <path>] [--keep-threshold <0-100>] [--flag-threshold <0-100>]"},{"name":"ui-jury-map","description":"Standalone Cartographer invocation that discovers app routes via bounded BFS crawl, producing a validated app-map.yaml. Auto-imports routes.yaml on first run with backup to routes.yaml.v3-backup. Runs preflight, validates URL, handles migration, spawns Cartographer agent, validates outputs, reports summary. (user)","argumentHint":"<dev-url>"},{"name":"save-project","description":"Snapshot the current session before deploy: bump semver versions for the changed side(s), commit with a structured message, and push. Pairs with `task prod:deploy` to keep dev and prod versions diffable via `/api/v1/health`. (project)","argumentHint":"Optional: any additional notes or context about the current changes"},{"name":"deep-research","description":"Deep research harness — fan-out web searches, fetch sources, adversarially verify claims, synthesize a cited report. (dynamic workflow)","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"design","description":"Grant or revoke Claude agent access to your Design projects","argumentHint":"consent | revoke","builtin":true},{"name":"design-sync","description":"Push a React design system to claude.ai/design. This runs a converter that bundles the real component code (from Storybook or a bare package) and uploads it. Use when the user runs /design-sync or says \"sync my design system to Claude Design\".","argumentHint":"[<project hint, e.g. \"Acme DS\">]","builtin":true},{"name":"dataviz","description":"Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"update-config","description":"Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"verify","description":"Verify that a code change actually does what it's supposed to by exercising it end-to-end and observing behavior — drive the affected flow, not just tests or typecheck. Run before committing nontrivial changes; bootstraps this repo's project verify skill if none exists yet. Don't invoke it on a diff that only touches tests, docs, or other code with no runtime surface to drive (a change to product source always has one) — there's nothing to observe.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"debug","description":"Enable debug logging for this session and help diagnose issues","argumentHint":"[issue description]","builtin":true},{"name":"code-review","description":"Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs (plus reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups where the model's review recipe covers them) at the given effort level (low|medium|high|xhigh|max: from few high-confidence findings up to many, some of them uncertain; ultra: deep multi-agent review in the cloud); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review. Pass --max-findings <n> to report up to n findings, or --max-findings all for every finding. The choice stays until you pass --max-findings default. For ultra on a GitHub.com PR target, --post asks to post the finished review’s findings to the PR as a single comment from the user’s GitHub account (not a review; the launch dialog still confirms in interactive sessions, while non-interactive mode posts on the flag alone) and --no-post hides that option.","argumentHint":"[low|medium|high|xhigh|max|ultra] [--fix] [--comment] [<pr#>|<branch>|<path>] [--max-findings <n>|all]","aliases":["review"],"builtin":true},{"name":"simplify","description":"Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.","argumentHint":"[<target>]","builtin":true},{"name":"batch","description":"Research and plan a large-scale change, then execute it in parallel across 5–30 isolated worktree agents that each open a PR.","argumentHint":"<instruction>","builtin":true},{"name":"fewer-permission-prompts","description":"Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"doctor","description":"Health-check the user's Claude Code setup and fix issues: diagnose installation health — what the `claude doctor` terminal diagnostics cover — from local data (duplicate or leftover installs, PATH, unparseable settings files, broken or colliding agent definitions, skills whose frontmatter fails to parse); find unused skills, MCP servers, and plugins versus their context cost and disable dead weight; deduplicate local CLAUDE.md files against checked-in ones; trim checked-in CLAUDE.md files by cutting content a session could derive from the codebase (directory layouts, tech-stack lists, architecture overviews) while keeping gotchas, rationale, and non-standard conventions; migrate always-loaded CLAUDE.md guidance into lazy skills and nested CLAUDE.md files; flag slow hooks and context-heavy extensions; check the installed version is current; make auto mode the default permission mode; and pre-approve frequently denied read-only commands. Use when the user asks for a doctor run, checkup, audit, tune-up, or cleanup of their Claude Code setup or configuration.","argumentHint":"[prompt-audit [<path>]]","aliases":["checkup"],"builtin":true},{"name":"loop","description":"Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace.","argumentHint":"[interval] [prompt]","aliases":["proactive"],"builtin":true},{"name":"schedule","description":"Create, update, list, or run scheduled cloud agents (routines) that execute on a cron schedule.","argumentHint":"","aliases":["routines"],"builtin":true},{"name":"claude-api","description":"Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"workflow-authoring","description":"Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"run","description":"Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"run-skill-generator","description":"Author or improve the run-<unit> skill - a per-project skill that tells agents how to build, launch, and drive this project's app. Use when the user asks to set up the project, get it running, write run instructions, or verify build/run steps work from a clean environment.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"plugin-authoring","description":"Make a mod: a live pane, band, status line, toast or hook inside Claude Code (terminal or desktop Code tab), written as a plugin of function hooks that hot-reloads in this session. Load before writing or debugging a hooks module.","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"advisor","description":"Let Claude consult a stronger model at key moments","argumentHint":"[fable|opus|sonnet|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"agents","description":"(removed) Ask Claude to create/manage subagents, or edit .claude/agents/","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"auto-mode-setup","description":"Teach auto mode about your environment, plus optional rule tweaks","argumentHint":"[--request-id <uuid>] (--wizard posture=… scope=… depth=… --propose | --expect-sha256 <64-hex> --apply-file <path>)","builtin":true},{"name":"autocompact","description":"Configure the auto-compact window size","argumentHint":"[auto|<tokens>]","builtin":true},{"name":"clear","description":"Start a new session with empty context; previous session stays on disk (resumable with /resume)","argumentHint":"[name]","aliases":["reset","new"],"builtin":true},{"name":"color","description":"Set the prompt bar color for this session","argumentHint":"[red|blue|green|yellow|purple|orange|pink|cyan|default]","builtin":true},{"name":"compact","description":"Free up context by summarizing the conversation so far","argumentHint":"<optional custom summarization instructions>","builtin":true},{"name":"config","description":"Set a setting by key","argumentHint":"key=value","aliases":["settings"],"builtin":true},{"name":"output-style","description":"List output styles or switch to one","argumentHint":"[style]","builtin":true},{"name":"context","description":"Show current context usage","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"effort","description":"Set effort level for model usage","argumentHint":"<low|medium|high|xhigh|max|auto|ultracode [on|off]>","builtin":true},{"name":"fast","description":"Toggle fast mode (Opus 5.5)","argumentHint":"[on|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"focus","description":"Toggle focus view: just your prompt, summary, and response","argumentHint":"[on|off]","builtin":true},{"name":"heapdump","description":"Dump the JS heap to the Desktop; on Linux with no Desktop folder, the home directory","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"init","description":"Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"mcp","description":"Manage MCP servers","argumentHint":"[reconnect|enable|disable [<server>|all]]","builtin":true},{"name":"import","description":"Import config from another AI coding agent","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"model","description":"Set the AI model for Claude Code","argumentHint":"<model>","builtin":true},{"name":"__remote-workflow","description":"Run the workflow script delivered in this session environment (server-launched sessions only)","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"workflow-launch-exec","description":"Execute a server-launched workflow handoff (workflow_launch event sessions only)","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"reload-plugins","description":"Activate pending plugin changes in the current session","argumentHint":"[--force]","builtin":true},{"name":"reload-skills","description":"Pick up skills added or changed on disk during this session","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"rename","description":"Rename the current conversation","argumentHint":"[name]","aliases":["name"],"builtin":true},{"name":"ultrareview","description":"Start a cloud agent that finds and verifies bugs in your branch (~15-25 min, $5-$25 USD) · Runs in a cloud session. See https://code.claude.com/docs/en/claude-code-on-the-web","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"security-review","description":"Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"usage-credits","description":"Configure usage credits or request them from your admin when you hit a limit","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"extra-usage","description":"Renamed to /usage-credits","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"usage","description":"Show session cost, plan usage, and what's contributing to your limits","argumentHint":"","aliases":["cost","stats"],"builtin":true},{"name":"insights","description":"Generate a report analyzing your Claude Code sessions","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"recap","description":"Generate a one-line session recap now","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"skill-doctor","description":"Show which loaded skills are unused and costing context","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"goal","description":"Set a goal — keep working until the condition is met","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"design-consent","description":"Grant Claude agent access to your Design projects","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"design-revoke","description":"Revoke Claude agent access to your Design projects","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"list-agents","description":"List subagents, teammates, and other Claude sessions you can message","argumentHint":"","aliases":["peers"],"builtin":true},{"name":"team-onboarding","description":"Help teammates ramp on Claude Code with a guide from your usage","argumentHint":"","builtin":true},{"name":"docs","description":"docs (editable docs people share and comment on; the default for any document, named as a doc or not: a document, report, proposal, resume, cover letter, letter, contract, policy, form, template, worksheet, essay, handbook, guide, how-to, cheat sheet, SOP or other writing to keep, share, collaborate on, send, submit, print or sign; a doc exports to Word, PDF, Markdown or Google Docs, so needing a file to send, attach, upload, submit or print is no reason to pick Word, and a file nobody asked for is a doc, not Word; a plan, comparison, summary or notes asked in chat stays in chat; a pasted claude.ai artifact link may be a doc: check with docs tools first; Word or another file format named, tracked changes wanted, or a .docx to change or use as a template → that format's skill): making one → if no docs-connector instructions are in context, call its `guide` (topic.instructions) first; then create the doc (headings only, no body) before any search, file read or plan, even with files attached. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:docs"]},{"name":"docx","description":"Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, read, edit, or manipulate Word documents (.docx) or Word templates (.dotx). Triggers include: any mention of Microsoft Word Documents, such as 'Word doc', 'word document', '.docx', '.dotx', 'microsoft doc'. Also use when extracting or reorganizing content from .docx or .dotx files, inserting or replacing images in documents, find-and-replace in Word files, working with tracked changes or comments, or converting content into a polished Word document. If the user asks for a deliverable as a Word or .docx file (to download, email or print), use this skill. However, if they ask for a document, page, report, memo, or notes WITHOUT naming a file format and the session offers Claude's own dedicated document or page skill or connector, use that instead, even if they will email or print it. Do NOT use for PDFs, spreadsheets, Google Docs, or coding unrelated to document generation. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:docx"]},{"name":"google-workspace","description":"Read this before the first Google Drive, Docs, Sheets or Slides connector call whenever the task creates or changes a Google file. Use this skill whenever the user wants to create or change a Google Doc, Sheet or Slides file in their Google Drive. Triggers include: a request that names Google Docs, Sheets, Slides or Drive and asks to make, edit, format, copy or rename a file; a docs.google.com link with a request to change that file, even a one-line fix or suggested edits; and any follow-up change to a Google file from earlier in the chat, even \"change it\" or \"add a tab\". Includes helper scripts for document positions, cell ranges and slide layout. However, if the user asks for a doc, deck or spreadsheet without naming Google, or gives a Google file only as source material for something new, use Claude's own output type instead. Do NOT use for read-only questions about a Google file, or for Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF files. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:google-workspace"]},{"name":"import-memory","description":"Import a memory export from another AI assistant into Claude's memory — conversationally, additively, and with the content treated as data. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:import-memory"]},{"name":"morning","description":"Render the user's morning brief as a styled HTML artifact, or set it up as a recurring weekday task. Use only when the user explicitly asks to run, see, or set up their morning brief, or if they invoke /morning by name. A question about their day, schedule, or calendar is not by itself a request for the brief; answer it directly instead. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:morning"]},{"name":"pdf","description":"Use this skill whenever the user wants to do anything with PDF files. This includes reading or extracting text/tables from PDFs, combining or merging multiple PDFs into one, splitting PDFs apart, rotating pages, adding watermarks, creating new PDFs, filling PDF forms, encrypting/decrypting PDFs, extracting images, and OCR on scanned PDFs to make them searchable. If the user mentions a .pdf file or asks to produce one, use this skill. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:pdf"]},{"name":"pptx","description":"Use this skill any time a .pptx or .potx file is involved in any way — as input, output, or both. This includes: creating slide decks, pitch decks, or presentations as PowerPoint (.pptx) files; reading, parsing, or extracting text from any .pptx or .potx file (even if the extracted content will be used elsewhere, like in an email, summary, or creating a different type of slide deck); editing, modifying, or updating existing presentations; combining or splitting slide files; working with templates (.potx), layouts, speaker notes, or comments. Trigger whenever the user asks for a PowerPoint or .pptx file, or references a .pptx or .potx filename, regardless of what they plan to do with the content afterward. However, when the user asks for a deck, slides, a slide deck, or a presentation without naming a file format, default to using a dedicated slide-deck artifact type or a separate slides skill if this session offers one; otherwise, use this skill. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:pptx"]},{"name":"skill-creator","description":"Create new skills, modify and improve existing skills, and measure skill performance. Use when users want to create a skill from scratch, edit, or optimize an existing skill, run evals to test a skill, benchmark skill performance with variance analysis, or optimize a skill's description for better triggering accuracy. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:skill-creator"]},{"name":"xlsx","description":"Use this skill any time a spreadsheet file is the primary input or output. This means any task where the user wants to: open, read, edit, or fix an existing .xlsx, .xlsm, .xltx, .csv, or .tsv file (e.g., adding columns, computing formulas, formatting, charting, cleaning messy data); create a new spreadsheet from scratch or from other data sources; or convert between tabular file formats. Trigger especially when the user references a spreadsheet file by name or path — even casually (like \"the xlsx in my downloads\") — and wants something done to it or produced from it. Also trigger for cleaning or restructuring messy tabular data files (malformed rows, misplaced headers, junk data) into proper spreadsheets. The deliverable must be a spreadsheet file. Do NOT trigger when the primary deliverable is a Word document, HTML report, standalone Python script, database pipeline, or Google Sheets API integration, even if tabular data is involved. (claude.ai sync)","argumentHint":"","aliases":["anthropic-skills:xlsx"]},{"name":"claude.ai Todoist:productivity-analysis (MCP)","description":"Analyze your Todoist productivity with insights on completion trends, goal streaks, project distribution, and actionable recommendations. Gathers data from multiple tools and synthesizes a comprehensive report.","argumentHint":""}],"uuid":"96a2192f-4507-4c14-9f1f-47dac9ddd346","session_id":"bef3f2da-be92-470f-abe2-ea78c8e52171"}
