{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BWnrY2Xrw6oyDYQkN6trGf","type":"tool_result","content":"0084_toast_orders_reconciliation.sql\n0085_subscribers.sql\n0086_merge_repoint_and_erasure_backstop.sql\n0087_qr_scans_dedupe_bucket.sql\nefcd970 docs: run 20261006 — two work orders lifted from the signed slate, own run-loop lane\nbf89a26 docs: slate-20261007 signed off — 4 cards, Activity K (review leftovers + Inventory Setup races) on the run loop, after 20261006 (ledger T-69)\n8e4ee1c docs: slate-20261006 signed off — 2 cards, Activity E on the run loop (ledger T-68)\n7d252ce docs: SignalWire replaces Twilio as the delivery provider (decision 208, ledger T-67)\na6c89aa docs: triage-20261005 receipt — push line stamped\nbackend/internal/marketing/subscribers.go\nbackend/internal/marketing/subscribers_test.go\nls: cannot access '.night-crew/runs/20261006': No such file or directory\n.night-crew/runs/2026-10-06-autonomous/:\ndispatch\nlogs\ntimings.log\nTonight's autonomous run. Execute the signed slate at reference/slate-20261006.md\n(`.night-crew/knowledge/reference/slate-20261006.md` in this repo).\n\nSetup: you are on branch dev.\n\nBefore any git write, CHECK FOR UNMERGED RUN BRANCHES. Run this EXACTLY as written —\nit contains no placeholder to substitute, and it asks about every date, because an\nunfinished night from any date is a night's work sitting unmerged:\n\n    git branch --no-merged dev --format='%(refname:short)' | grep -E '^overnight-[0-9]{8}(-[0-9]+)?$'\n\nSTATE THE RESULT OUT LOUD BEFORE PROCEEDING — either \"no unmerged run branches\" or the\nlist. You may only proceed after saying which one you saw. An empty result is the \"go\"\nsignal, so a command that failed or ran wrong looks exactly like a clean repo unless\nyou have named what you found.\n\n- No unmerged run branch → say so, then cut this run's branch. Compute its name from\n  the BARE DATE, since you are looking for every branch of that date:\n\n      git branch --format='%(refname:short)' | grep -E '^overnight-20261006(-[0-9]+)?$' | sort -V\n\n  No match → overnight-20261006. Otherwise the next free suffix (-2, -3, …). A MERGED\n  branch's name is still taken, so a date whose run merged this morning yields -2 —\n  cut it and proceed without asking; nothing is at risk.\n- ANY unmerged run branch exists → 🛑 STOP. A previous night's work is sitting on it.\n  Report what is there (git log --oneline dev..<branch>, and whether\n  DECISIONS-NEEDED.md carries an open section for it), then ASK THE OPERATOR to choose:\n  RESUME that branch, or RECUT at the next free suffix. Do NOT pick for them — resuming\n  mixes two nights on one branch, recutting walks away from a night's work, and you\n  cannot tell from here which is right. This is NOT covered by the batch sign-off: the\n  yes is scoped to the signed specs, and what to do with an unfinished night is not one\n  of them. Whatever they answer, the existing branch is left untouched — never delete,\n  reset, force, or rename it.\n\nIf the run id gains a suffix, EVERY run-scoped name takes it with them: the conflict\nlog reference/conflicts-<runid>.md, the run directory .night-crew/runs/2026-10-06-autonomous/,\nthe `--run-id` you pin on `night-crew run`, the scorecard record, and the closeout's\nreferences to the run. The slate keeps the name it was signed under.\n\nDo all work on the run branch. Never push, never touch main. Hard prohibitions in\nCLAUDE.md apply. 🛑 Tests run on :5434 (`yumyums-test-pg`, role `hqtest`), NEVER :5433 —\nthat is the dev AND production cluster (decision 155). The local `spike-supabase` substrate\nis brought up in RECONCILE mode only (`.night-crew/qa/spike-supabase/env-up.sh` with NO\nflags, never `--fresh`), graded on `echo EXIT=$?`, as the first act after the branch cut.\n`export PATH=\"/usr/local/go/bin:$PATH\"` before every Go or Playwright leg. 🛑 NO PROVIDER IS\nEVER CALLED: the `HQ_SW_*` env vars are unset in every gate and on this box; the sender is the\nrecording stub; a diff that reaches `*.signalwire.com` outside `internal/delivery`, or any\nreal send, is a stop.\n\nAuthority: batch sign-off was given by the operator on 2026-10-05 — the slate\ndocument is the record. Do NOT pause for per-change sign-off. Every operator fork is\nalready resolved in the ledger (T-68; decision 208; #10's hybrid payload; decision 187's\nprojection shape; decision 191 fail-closed), and you never choose a fork — what to build,\nwhat a milestone means, anything only the operator can settle.\n\nPark is NOT the default answer to a gray area. Route it through\n`night-crew decisions log` and act on the verdict it PRINTED, never on an inference\nfrom the disposition. Two verdicts, four producing states (CLAUDE.md §\"Slate workflow\"\nstep 5 is the doctrine of record):\n  - `verdict: decide` under an adopted delegation policy — recorded delegated,\n    `ratification: pending`, back at triage. Deciding is not settling.\n  - `verdict: decide` on the citation floor with NO policy involved — owes no\n    ratification. You will meet this; do not read `decide` as \"a policy covered it\".\n  - `verdict: park` with the decree read — prints the verdict and exits 0. An\n    escalation is a correct outcome, not a command failure.\n  - park because the decree could not be READ — fails, exits non-zero, renders `park:`\n    inside its error, and prints NO `verdict:` line at all. Grepping for\n    `verdict: park` misses this one; read the exit code.\nAn escalation and a park are the same act, which is why there are two verdicts and not\nthree. A delegation converts a question that WOULD have escalated into one that\nresolves; it is not the only way a question resolves — never restate this as \"decide\nonly under a policy, otherwise park\".\n\nRules of engagement:\n- Standing rules and verification gates G1–G6 are inherited unchanged from the\n  night-crew clone's reference/overnight-run-plan-20260707.md. There is no G5; the G4\n  discipline greps are N/A-VACUOUS in this repo (B-14). G4's precache count tonight is\n  51 and must still be 51 at every merge — Card 1 edits `marketing.html` (one line) and\n  Card 2 edits `marketing/subscribers.js`; each regenerates `sw.js` (`task sw`) in its own\n  change set; no card adds or removes a precached file.\n- Dispatch model — THIS NIGHT RIDES NIGHT-CREW'S PRODUCT RUN LOOP (decision 206, the\n  operator's call at triage T-66: \"from the next launch on, the night drives the run\n  loop\"). You are the control loop; you never implement a card in your own context. A\n  card becomes a WORK ORDER, not a worktree. Lift each card out of the slate's \"Work\n  order\" sections into the card-list shape dispatch consumes — slug, title, team\n  (`engineering`), complexity (`new-mechanism`), and the four authored sections Intent,\n  Spec, Gates, Context, verbatim from the slate's Card 1 and Card 2 entries. Gates must be\n  non-empty; dispatch refuses an unattended work order with no gates, and it is right to.\n  Result is loop-owned — never author a Result section. Then:\n\n      night-crew dispatch --cards <cards.json> --out <dir>\n      night-crew run --repo . --run-id 20261006 --wo <identity-code-and-qr.md> --wo <mms-send-on-signup.md>\n\n  PIN THE RUN ID AND COMPLETE THE WHOLE SLATE IN ONE `run` INVOCATION. Per-card `run`\n  calls with default run ids leave the closeout unable to find a summary and `loop\n  check` refuses; per-card calls sharing one `--run-id` make each card overwrite the\n  summary so every earlier card reads OFF LOOP. Exactly one shape works and it is the\n  one above (B-412). Say, before the first card goes out, that you are driving the loop\n  and where the run's records will be written. Before dispatch: `night-crew workers\n  check` (queues must be clear of stale pollers — they were at the sitting) and Temporal\n  liveness (started from the night-crew clone's `task temporal:dev`; hq's Taskfile has no\n  such target); the /nc-run §2a check governs.\n\n  THE WATCHDOG: start the `night-crew run` invocation IN THE BACKGROUND, never as a\n  blocking foreground call. Poll roughly every 10 minutes and say what you saw: new\n  commits on the run branch, movement in the run's records under .night-crew/runs/, the\n  worker log's tail. NO FORWARD PROGRESS FOR ~45 MINUTES → the watchdog fires: capture\n  whatever trail exists (worker log tail, journal tail, `night-crew loop check` output)\n  into the conflict log and the closeout, and PARK the remainder with the failure named.\n  THE RECOVERY LADDER (the /nc-run skill's) is the whole of what you may do when the loop\n  cannot carry a card: repair the work order's mechanics through the loop (re-dispatch,\n  retry, re-sequence); a repair that would change what the card builds is a fork and\n  parks; a broken card parks alone and the rest rides; a machinery-wide failure parks the\n  remainder. NEVER hand-run a card off the loop — a diff no run record can see is not a\n  card that completed (decision 206's stated cost: the first loop night here is unproven,\n  and a parked night is the honest outcome). The watchdog cannot catch the session itself\n  dying; that half of B-425 stays open and is disclosed in the slate.\n\n  G6 is still yours: when the loop reports a work order complete, spawn a SEPARATE fresh\n  subagent on the session model for the G6 adversarial review of its result before it\n  counts as landed; a fix round after review is diff-checked AND re-run through the test\n  it answers, stated in the conflict log. Subagents cannot spawn subagents — G6 dispatch is\n  always yours. A failed card PARKS per the slate rules (work preserved,\n  DECISIONS-NEEDED.md). Editing a file outside the card's footprint is NOT a park\n  (§15ad.65) — see the conflict rules below.\n- Order: SERIAL, one track, no concurrency — Card 1 `identity-code-and-qr` strictly\n  first (Card 2 consumes its mint; a Card 1 park parks Card 2 with it, named), then Card 2\n  `mms-send-on-signup`. Both ride the ONE `run` invocation above in that order. Opening\n  acts after the branch cut: `env-up.sh` reconcile (graded), `workers check` + Temporal,\n  then the base-reds leg (full Playwright on the detached `dev` base + Go base counts,\n  under `flock /tmp/hq-full-suite.lock`, the config's retries, say which) running\n  concurrently with the loop's first work order. You alone merge to the run branch, in\n  slate order, regenerating `sw.js` at every merged HEAD and checking idempotence. Lock\n  plan: base-reds → Card 1's FULL Playwright suite on its merged tree (it moves the\n  precache) → Card 2's FULL suite on the complete tree = the FINAL suite (priced at 55 m\n  each) → isolation of any red outside base, 3–5× alone on BOTH trees (~30 m). Confined\n  gates inside each work order are the slate's Gates sections.\n- Box rules (paste into EVERY work-order Context and every G6 prompt): per card its own\n  `TEST_PORT` and `TEST_DB_NAME=hq_test_e2e_<card>_20261006` (the reset guard's pattern)\n  and its own Go database `hq_test_go_<card>` on :5434 for `DB_TEST_URL` (`go test ./...\n  -p 1` is load-bearing; set `DB_TEST_URL` or the Go suite exits 0 while skipping every DB\n  test — check counts, not `ok`). One full Playwright suite on the box at a time, under\n  `flock /tmp/hq-full-suite.lock`; every full Go suite takes the SAME lock (B-460 — the\n  `internal/sync` RLS leg reads the substrate); any `TestRowVisibilityRLS` red is re-run\n  alone before it is reported, both exit lines stated. 🛑 B-477: a fresh worktree reds\n  `[TI-01]` unless THREE symlinks are made before the first Playwright leg —\n  `node_modules`, `marketing/sync/harness/node_modules`,\n  `.night-crew/qa/spike-supabase/rxdb/node_modules` — each pointing at the main tree's;\n  then warm the backend build (`cd backend && go build -o /dev/null ./cmd/server/`)\n  BEFORE Playwright's 60 s webServer window. If `webServer` wedges, hand-provision the","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bef3f2