his box; the sender is the\nrecording stub; a diff that reaches `*.signalwire.com` outside `internal/delivery`, or any\nreal send, is a stop.\n\nAuthority: batch sign-off was given by the operator on 2026-10-05 — the slate\ndocument is the record. Do NOT pause for per-change sign-off. Every operator fork is\nalready resolved in the ledger (T-68; decision 208; #10's hybrid payload; decision 187's\nprojection shape; decision 191 fail-closed), and you never choose a fork — what to build,\nwhat a milestone means, anything only the operator can settle.\n\nPark is NOT the default answer to a gray area. Route it through\n`night-crew decisions log` and act on the verdict it PRINTED, never on an inference\nfrom the disposition. Two verdicts, four producing states (CLAUDE.md §\"Slate workflow\"\nstep 5 is the doctrine of record):\n  - `verdict: decide` under an adopted delegation policy — recorded delegated,\n    `ratification: pending`, back at triage. Deciding is not settling.\n  - `verdict: decide` on the citation floor with NO policy involved — owes no\n    ratification. You will meet this; do not read `decide` as \"a policy covered it\".\n  - `verdict: park` with the decree read — prints the verdict and exits 0. An\n    escalation is a correct outcome, not a command failure.\n  - park because the decree could not be READ — fails, exits non-zero, renders `park:`\n    inside its error, and prints NO `verdict:` line at all. Grepping for\n    `verdict: park` misses this one; read the exit code.\nAn escalation and a park are the same act, which is why there are two verdicts and not\nthree. A delegation converts a question that WOULD have escalated into one that\nresolves; it is not the only way a question resolves — never restate this as \"decide\nonly under a policy, otherwise park\".\n\nRules of engagement:\n- Standing rules and verification gates G1–G6 are inherited unchanged from the\n  night-crew clone's reference/overnight-run-plan-20260707.md. There is no G5; the G4\n 